# user/xsnarfd.ad — ONE clipboard across the namespace boundary.
#
# THE PROBLEM
# ===========
# /dev/snarf and /dev/snarf.primary are served (user/linux-snarf.c), so copy
# and paste work between HAMNIX programs: the editor, Notes, the browser URL
# bar and the grid terminal all reach the same two buffers by name. A Debian or
# Alpine binary gets ENOENT on those names -- measured, tests/linux/
# snarf_device.sh arm 3 -- because they are served INSIDE the process by a path
# interception in the Hamnix runtime, exactly as /dev/wsys, /net and /fd are.
# A foreign binary has no Hamnix runtime, so it uses what it has always used:
# X SELECTIONS, owned by the Xwayland inside its namespace.
#
# That is two clipboards. Copy a URL in Firefox, paste in the Hamnix editor:
# nothing. Copy in the editor, paste in an xterm inside the namespace: nothing.
#
# AN X SELECTION IS NOT A BUFFER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE DESIGN
# ========================================================
# There is no place in an X server where the clipboard lives. A client OWNS the
# selection; anyone pasting sends ConvertSelection and the OWNER answers with
# the bytes, in a format the paster asked for. So a bridge cannot poll a buffer
# on the X side and it cannot write one either. It has to BE a client:
#
#   * take ownership of CLIPBOARD and PRIMARY whenever /dev/snarf changes, and
#     answer every ConvertSelection out of the Hamnix buffer;
#   * notice when someone ELSE takes ownership, ask that owner for the bytes,
#     and write them into /dev/snarf.
#
# Both halves, or it is the success-shaped failure this tree exists to beat:
# a bridge that mirrors on copy but not on ownership change loses the paste
# that mattered and says nothing.
#
# WHERE IT RUNS, AND WHY THAT IS THE ONLY PLACE IT CAN
# ====================================================
# The two things it must touch are on opposite sides of a namespace boundary:
#
#   /dev/snarf          served in-process by the Hamnix runtime, over a shared
#                       segment whose name is $HAMSNARF, or $HAMWSYS + ".snarf"
#                       when a window system is pinned, or /srv/snarf. /srv is
#                       DELIBERATELY not carried into a subtree namespace, so
#                       none of those names exist inside one.
#   the X display       Xwayland runs INSIDE the distribution namespace, on
#                       :0, with its socket at that tree's /tmp/.X11-unix/X0.
#
# So it runs OUTSIDE the namespace, as root, next to the wsyswl that serves the
# same distribution -- and it reaches the display the Plan 9 way, BY NAME. The
# boot binds each distribution at /n/<name> (etc/rc.boot.linux,
# `bind '#distro/alpine' /n/alpine`), and that tree's /tmp is the medium's own,
# so the X socket a client inside sees as /tmp/.X11-unix/X0 is
# /n/alpine/tmp/.X11-unix/X0 from out here. Same inode, two names. Nothing is
# bound, nothing is copied, and /srv stays out of the namespace.
#
# ONE PER DISTRIBUTION, not one overall, for the same reason /etc/rc.distros-wl
# starts one wsyswl per distribution: there is one Xwayland per distribution and
# an X connection is to one server. They all share the one Hamnix clipboard,
# which is what makes it ONE clipboard: text copied in Debian's Firefox lands in
# /dev/snarf, and the Alpine bridge then claims Alpine's CLIPBOARD with it. The
# convergence is content-based, so it terminates instead of ringing.
#
# The socket path is an ARGUMENT, exactly as wsyswl's is -- no distribution name
# is compiled into this file.
#
#     xsnarfd /n/alpine/tmp/.X11-unix/X0 alpine
#
# WHY THE X11 WIRE PROTOCOL BY HAND
# =================================
# Because the alternative is not available: linking libX11 would make this a
# foreign binary, and a foreign binary is exactly the thing that cannot open
# /dev/snarf. The bridge has to be a Hamnix binary to have a Hamnix clipboard,
# so it speaks X11 on the wire. user/x11/xfill.ad already does that against the
# in-tree toy server; this talks to a real Xwayland, so nothing here may assume
# the toy server's fixed resource IDs -- the root window and the resource-id
# base are read out of the connection setup reply, as they must be.
#
# WHAT IT ANSWERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM TO
# ==============================================
# TARGETS, UTF8_STRING, STRING and TEXT, and TARGETS lists exactly those four.
# TIMESTAMP is REFUSED (SelectionNotify with property None) rather than listed
# and answered with a lie: getting a real ownership timestamp needs the
# zero-length-property-append round trip, this does not do it, and advertising a
# target one cannot answer is the same class of defect as a device path with no
# server behind it. TEXT is answered with type UTF8_STRING, which is what ICCCM
# asks of an owner that chose UTF-8; STRING is answered with the same bytes,
# which is what every clipboard tool in practice does with UTF-8 text.
#
# THE 64 KiB CAP IS THE DEVICE'S AND IS NOT WIDENED HERE. An X selection larger
# than SNARF_MAX is TRUNCATED at 65536 bytes and said so, loudly, by name and
# size -- the same rule /dev/snarf itself applies to a 70 000-byte write from a
# Hamnix program (docs/linux_clipboard.md §4). An owner that answers with an
# INCR transfer is REFUSED loudly and the Hamnix clipboard is LEFT ALONE, which
# is the honest answer: a half-received INCR stream is a corrupt paste.
#
# HOW A CHANGE ON THE HAMNIX SIDE IS NOTICED: BY CONTENT, and that is a gap
# worth naming. struct snarfshm has magic and version but NO serial or
# generation counter, so there is nothing to compare cheaply and nothing to
# wait on. This polls both buffers four times a second and compares bytes.
# It is correct (a change is a change) and it costs a 128 KiB memcmp per
# second, but it cannot distinguish "written again with the same bytes" from
# "not written", and it cannot wake instantly. A `uint64 serial` bumped in
# hamsnarf_write would let this compare one word and would let a future
# sys_waitfds park on the clipboard; see HANDOFF for the exact request.
#
# Adder quirks obeyed here: every buffer is a top-level BSS array (not a frame
# local), there is no adjacent string-literal concatenation, and no string
# literal is assigned into a global array.

extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_write(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_waitfds(fds: Ptr[int32], nfds: uint64, timeout_ms: int64) -> int64
extern def sys_unix_connect(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_exit(code: uint64)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- constants
SNARF_MAX: uint64 = 65536

# X11 core request opcodes.
OP_CreateWindow:       uint8 = 1
OP_InternAtom:         uint8 = 16
OP_ChangeProperty:     uint8 = 18
OP_GetProperty:        uint8 = 20
OP_SetSelectionOwner:  uint8 = 22
OP_GetSelectionOwner:  uint8 = 23
OP_ConvertSelection:   uint8 = 24
OP_SendEvent:          uint8 = 25
OP_QueryExtension:     uint8 = 98

# X11 message type bytes.
MSG_Error:            uint8 = 0
MSG_Reply:            uint8 = 1
EV_PropertyNotify:    uint8 = 28
EV_SelectionClear:    uint8 = 29
EV_SelectionRequest:  uint8 = 30
EV_SelectionNotify:   uint8 = 31

# Predefined atoms (X11 protocol appendix B) -- these need no InternAtom.
ATOM_PRIMARY: uint32 = 1
ATOM_ATOM:    uint32 = 4
ATOM_STRING:  uint32 = 31

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- buffers
# All top-level, all BSS: this compiler cannot carry them as frame locals.
reqbuf:   Array[512, uint8]        # every fixed-size request is built here
xin:      Array[64, uint8]         # the 32-byte message header just read
xferbuf:  Array[65600, uint8]      # a reply's variable part / a pull's bytes
snbuf:    Array[65600, uint8]      # what /dev/snarf holds right now
clipbuf:  Array[65600, uint8]      # what BOTH sides agree CLIPBOARD holds
primbuf:  Array[65600, uint8]      # ditto PRIMARY
sinkbuf:  Array[1024, uint8]       # drains a reply longer than we asked for
padbuf:   Array[4, uint8]          # up to three zero pad bytes
numbuf:   Array[24, uint8]
sockpath: Array[512, uint8]
tagbuf:   Array[64, uint8]
waitset:  Array[4, int32]

clip_len: uint64 = 0
prim_len: uint64 = 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- X state
xfd:        int32  = -1
xdead:      int32  = 0            # the connection broke; drop it and redial
xseq:       uint32 = 0            # sequence number of the last request sent
root_win:   uint32 = 0
res_base:   uint32 = 0
res_mask:   uint32 = 0
max_req:    uint32 = 0            # maximum-request-length, in 4-byte units
mywin:      uint32 = 0

atom_clipboard: uint32 = 0
atom_utf8:      uint32 = 0
atom_targets:   uint32 = 0
atom_text:      uint32 = 0
atom_incr:      uint32 = 0
atom_prop:      uint32 = 0        # the property we ask owners to answer into

xfixes_op:    uint32 = 0          # XFIXES major opcode, 0 = not present
xfixes_event: uint32 = 0          # its first event code

own_clip: int32 = 0               # we are the X owner of CLIPBOARD
own_prim: int32 = 0               # ... and of PRIMARY

pull_clip: int32 = 0              # somebody else took it; go and ask them
pull_prim: int32 = 0

# The variable part of the reply x_await_reply() just consumed.
rep_extra:  uint64 = 0
rep_trunc:  int32  = 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- basics

def slen(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def puts(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, slen(s))


def putd(fd: int32, v: uint64):
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: uint64 = v
    if x == 0:
        numbuf[0] = 48
        n = 1
    while x > 0:
        numbuf[n] = cast[uint8](48 + x % 10)
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    i: uint64 = n
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        sys_write(fd, &numbuf[i], 1)


# Every line this program prints starts with its tag, so two bridges sharing a
# console can be told apart without reading the path.
def say(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    puts(1, "[xsnarfd ")
    puts(1, &tagbuf[0])
    puts(1, "] ")
    puts(1, s)


def sayerr(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    puts(2, "[xsnarfd ")
    puts(2, &tagbuf[0])
    puts(2, "] ")
    puts(2, s)


def put16(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: uint32):
    buf[at]     = cast[uint8](v & 255)
    buf[at + 1] = cast[uint8]((v >> 8) & 255)


def put32(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: uint32):
    buf[at]     = cast[uint8](v & 255)
    buf[at + 1] = cast[uint8]((v >> 8) & 255)
    buf[at + 2] = cast[uint8]((v >> 16) & 255)
    buf[at + 3] = cast[uint8]((v >> 24) & 255)


def get16(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64) -> uint32:
    return cast[uint32](buf[at]) | (cast[uint32](buf[at + 1]) << 8)


def get32(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64) -> uint32:
    a: uint32 = cast[uint32](buf[at])
    b: uint32 = cast[uint32](buf[at + 1]) << 8
    c: uint32 = cast[uint32](buf[at + 2]) << 16
    d: uint32 = cast[uint32](buf[at + 3]) << 24
    return a | b | c | d


def pad4(n: uint64) -> uint64:
    r: uint64 = n % 4
    if r == 0:
        return n
    return n + 4 - r


def zero(buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        buf[i] = 0
        i = i + 1


def read_exact(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int32:
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < count:
        n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &buf[got], count - got)
        if n <= 0:
            return 0
        got = got + cast[uint64](n)
    return 1


def send_all(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int32:
    sent: uint64 = 0
    while sent < count:
        n: int64 = sys_write(fd, &buf[sent], count - sent)
        if n <= 0:
            return 0
        sent = sent + cast[uint64](n)
    return 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the wire

def x_send(buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> uint32:
    # EVERY request bumps the sequence number, including the ones with no
    # reply -- the server counts them all, and a reply matched against a
    # sequence that skipped the silent requests matches the wrong thing.
    xseq = xseq + 1
    if send_all(xfd, buf, n) == 0:
        xdead = 1
    return xseq


# One message, or nothing. Returns 1 = a 32-byte header is in xin (and, for a
# reply, its variable part is in xferbuf), 0 = the timeout expired with the
# socket quiet, -1 = the server went away.
#
# THE VARIABLE PART IS ALWAYS DRAINED HERE, in one place, because a reply left
# half-read desynchronises the stream permanently and the symptom is an
# unrelated event arriving mangled minutes later.
def x_poll_msg(timeout_ms: int64) -> int32:
    if xfd < 0 or xdead != 0:
        return -1
    waitset[0] = xfd
    r: int64 = sys_waitfds(&waitset[0], 1, timeout_ms)
    if r <= 0:
        return 0
    if read_exact(xfd, &xin[0], 32) == 0:
        xdead = 1
        return -1
    rep_extra = 0
    rep_trunc = 0
    if xin[0] != MSG_Reply:
        return 1
    extra: uint64 = cast[uint64](get32(&xin[0], 4)) * 4
    take: uint64 = extra
    if take > 65600:
        take = 65600
        rep_trunc = 1
    if take > 0:
        if read_exact(xfd, &xferbuf[0], take) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return -1
    rep_extra = take
    left: uint64 = extra - take
    while left > 0:
        chunk: uint64 = left
        if chunk > 1024:
            chunk = 1024
        if read_exact(xfd, &sinkbuf[0], chunk) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return -1
        left = left - chunk
    return 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- events
# Declared before x_await_reply because a reply can arrive with events queued
# in front of it, and those events must be SERVED, not dropped: a paste in an
# X client is a SelectionRequest, and it can land while we are waiting for the
# reply to an InternAtom we sent a millisecond earlier.

def serve_request():
    when: uint32      = get32(&xin[0], 4)
    requestor: uint32 = get32(&xin[0], 12)
    sel: uint32       = get32(&xin[0], 16)
    target: uint32    = get32(&xin[0], 20)
    prop: uint32      = get32(&xin[0], 24)
    # An obsolete client sends property None and means "put it in the target".
    if prop == 0:
        prop = target

    src: Ptr[uint8] = &clipbuf[0]
    n: uint64 = clip_len
    okesel: int32 = 0
    if sel == atom_clipboard:
        okesel = 1
    elif sel == ATOM_PRIMARY:
        src = &primbuf[0]
        n = prim_len
        okesel = 1

    if okesel == 0:
        send_selection_notify(requestor, when, sel, target, 0)
        return

    if target == atom_targets:
        # EXACTLY the four we answer, in the order a paster reads them.
        zero(&reqbuf[0], 24)
        reqbuf[0] = OP_ChangeProperty
        reqbuf[1] = 0                       # mode = Replace
        put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 10)            # (24 + 16) / 4
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, requestor)
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, prop)
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, ATOM_ATOM)
        reqbuf[16] = 32                     # format
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 20, 4)            # four atoms
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 24, atom_targets)
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 28, atom_utf8)
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 32, ATOM_STRING)
        put32(&reqbuf[0], 36, atom_text)
        x_send(&reqbuf[0], 40)
        send_selection_notify(requestor, when, sel, target, prop)
        return

    typ: uint32 = 0
    if target == atom_utf8:
        typ = atom_utf8
    elif target == atom_text:
        # ICCCM: the owner picks the encoding for TEXT and REPORTS it in the
        # type. Ours is UTF-8, so say so rather than claiming STRING.
        typ = atom_utf8
    elif target == ATOM_STRING:
        typ = ATOM_STRING
    else:
        # TIMESTAMP, COMPOUND_TEXT, image/png, whatever else: we do not answer
        # it, so we REFUSE it. A property left unset with a SelectionNotify
        # that names it is the shape that makes a paster hang.
        send_selection_notify(requestor, when, sel, target, 0)
        return

    # The header goes out, then the bytes straight from the buffer, then the
    # pad -- no third copy of a 64 KiB clipboard just to concatenate them.
    total: uint64 = 24 + pad4(n)
    if cast[uint64](max_req) * 4 < total:
        sayerr("this X server's maximum request is too small for the clipboard; refusing rather than sending a truncated one\n")
        send_selection_notify(requestor, when, sel, target, 0)
        return
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 24)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_ChangeProperty
    reqbuf[1] = 0
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, requestor)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, prop)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, typ)
    reqbuf[16] = 8                          # format = 8 bits per unit
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 20, cast[uint32](n))
    xseq = xseq + 1
    if send_all(xfd, &reqbuf[0], 24) == 0:
        xdead = 1
        return
    if n > 0:
        if send_all(xfd, src, n) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return
    p: uint64 = pad4(n) - n
    if p > 0:
        zero(&padbuf[0], 4)
        if send_all(xfd, &padbuf[0], p) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return
    send_selection_notify(requestor, when, sel, target, prop)


def send_selection_notify(requestor: uint32, when: uint32, sel: uint32,
                          target: uint32, prop: uint32):
    # SendEvent: 12 bytes of request, then the 32-byte event.
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 44)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_SendEvent
    reqbuf[1] = 0                           # propagate = False
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 11)                # 44 / 4
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, requestor)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, 0)                 # event-mask 0: straight to them
    reqbuf[12] = EV_SelectionNotify
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 16, when)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 20, requestor)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 24, sel)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 28, target)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 32, prop)
    x_send(&reqbuf[0], 44)


def note_clear(sel: uint32):
    if sel == atom_clipboard:
        own_clip = 0
    elif sel == ATOM_PRIMARY:
        own_prim = 0


# THE SEND-EVENT BIT. An event a client sent with SendEvent rather than one
# the server generated arrives with the top bit of the type byte SET -- so a
# SelectionNotify from an owner answering our ConvertSelection is type 159, not
# 31, and every ICCCM answer in the protocol comes that way because SendEvent
# is how an owner replies. Comparing the raw byte cost one measured run in
# which the Hamnix->X direction worked perfectly and X->Hamnix timed out three
# seconds at a time with no error anywhere: the message was arriving, being
# recognised as nothing, and being dropped.
def evtype() -> uint8:
    return xin[0] & 127


# Every message that is not the reply we are waiting for lands here.
def dispatch():
    t: uint8 = evtype()
    if t == EV_SelectionRequest:
        serve_request()
        return
    if t == EV_SelectionClear:
        sel: uint32 = get32(&xin[0], 12)
        note_clear(sel)
        # ALWAYS armed, XFixes or not. Without XFixes this is the only notice
        # we get that the clipboard changed hands. With XFixes it is a second
        # notice of the same handover -- and it stays armed deliberately,
        # because XFixesSelectSelectionInput has NO REPLY, so a bridge that
        # relied on it alone would go silently one-way if that request were
        # ever refused (which is exactly what a wrong length field did here
        # once). Both paths set the same flag; the pull happens once.
        if sel == atom_clipboard:
            pull_clip = 1
        elif sel == ATOM_PRIMARY:
            pull_prim = 1
        return
    if xfixes_op != 0 and cast[uint32](t) == xfixes_event:
        owner: uint32 = get32(&xin[0], 8)
        fsel: uint32  = get32(&xin[0], 12)
        # Our OWN claim comes back as an event too. Acting on it would ask
        # ourselves for the bytes we just published.
        if owner == mywin:
            return
        if owner == 0:
            # Nobody owns it now -- the last owner exited. There is no content
            # to fetch and no reason to throw ours away, so keep what we have
            # and say what happened.
            note_clear(fsel)
            say("the X selection lost its owner; keeping the last content\n")
            return
        note_clear(fsel)
        if fsel == atom_clipboard:
            pull_clip = 1
        elif fsel == ATOM_PRIMARY:
            pull_prim = 1
        return
    if t == MSG_Error:
        # An error for a request we are not waiting on. Name it -- a silent
        # BadWindow here is how a bridge ends up serving nobody.
        sayerr("X error code ")
        putd(2, cast[uint64](xin[1]))
        puts(2, " for request opcode ")
        putd(2, cast[uint64](xin[10]))
        puts(2, "\n")
        return
    # PropertyNotify (we ask for no property events), Expose on nothing,
    # MappingNotify: ignored on purpose.


# Wait for the reply to request `seq`, serving whatever arrives first.
# 1 = the reply is in xin (+ xferbuf), 0 = an X error or the wait timed out.
def x_await_reply(seq: uint32) -> int32:
    budget: int32 = 50                     # 50 x 100 ms = five seconds
    while budget > 0:
        r: int32 = x_poll_msg(100)
        if r < 0:
            return 0
        if r == 0:
            budget = budget - 1
            continue
        s: uint32 = get16(&xin[0], 2)
        if xin[0] == MSG_Reply and s == (seq & 65535):
            return 1
        if xin[0] == MSG_Error and s == (seq & 65535):
            sayerr("X error code ")
            putd(2, cast[uint64](xin[1]))
            puts(2, " replying to opcode ")
            putd(2, cast[uint64](xin[10]))
            puts(2, "\n")
            return 0
        dispatch()
    sayerr("timed out waiting for an X reply\n")
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- setup

def x_connect(path: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_unix_connect(cast[Ptr[char]](path))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    xfd = fd
    xdead = 0
    xseq = 0

    # Connection setup: 'l', major 11, minor 0, no authorisation. Xwayland on
    # this line is started with no -auth (etc/de-ns-run.linux,
    # scripts/hamlinux_alpine.sh), so an empty authorisation is what it wants;
    # a server that wanted a cookie would refuse here and say so below.
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 12)
    reqbuf[0] = 108
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 11)
    if send_all(xfd, &reqbuf[0], 12) == 0:
        sayerr("could not send the X connection setup\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0
    if read_exact(xfd, &xin[0], 8) == 0:
        sayerr("no X setup reply\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0
    if xin[0] != 1:
        sayerr("the X server REFUSED the connection (it wants authorisation this bridge does not send)\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0
    body: uint64 = cast[uint64](get16(&xin[0], 6)) * 4
    if body > 65600:
        sayerr("X setup reply is implausibly large\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0
    if read_exact(xfd, &xferbuf[0], body) == 0:
        sayerr("short X setup reply\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0

    # Offsets are from the start of the BODY (protocol offset 8).
    res_base = get32(&xferbuf[0], 4)
    res_mask = get32(&xferbuf[0], 8)
    max_req  = get16(&xferbuf[0], 18)
    vendor_len: uint64 = cast[uint64](get16(&xferbuf[0], 16))
    nscreens: uint64   = cast[uint64](xferbuf[20])
    nformats: uint64   = cast[uint64](xferbuf[21])
    # vendor string, then nformats x 8 bytes, then the SCREENs. The first four
    # bytes of the first screen are its root window -- which is the parent our
    # window needs, and is NOT a constant however many toy servers say 1.
    off: uint64 = 32 + pad4(vendor_len) + nformats * 8
    if nscreens == 0 or off + 4 > body:
        sayerr("X setup reply has no screen\n")
        x_drop()
        return 0
    root_win = get32(&xferbuf[0], off)

    say("connected: root window 0x")
    putd(1, cast[uint64](root_win))
    puts(1, ", resource base 0x")
    putd(1, cast[uint64](res_base))
    puts(1, ", max request ")
    putd(1, cast[uint64](max_req) * 4)
    puts(1, " bytes\n")
    return 1


def x_drop():
    if xfd >= 0:
        sys_close(xfd)
    xfd = -1
    xdead = 0
    mywin = 0
    own_clip = 0
    own_prim = 0
    pull_clip = 0
    pull_prim = 0
    xfixes_op = 0
    xfixes_event = 0


def intern_atom(name: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint32:
    n: uint64 = slen(name)
    total: uint64 = 8 + pad4(n)
    zero(&reqbuf[0], total)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_InternAtom
    reqbuf[1] = 0                          # only-if-exists = False
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 4, cast[uint32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        reqbuf[8 + i] = name[i]
        i = i + 1
    seq: uint32 = x_send(&reqbuf[0], total)
    if x_await_reply(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    return get32(&xin[0], 8)


def create_window() -> int32:
    mywin = res_base | 1
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 32)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_CreateWindow
    reqbuf[1] = 0                          # depth 0: CopyFromParent
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 8)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, mywin)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, root_win)
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 12, 0)               # x
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 14, 0)               # y
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 16, 1)               # width
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 18, 1)               # height
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 20, 0)               # border width
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 22, 2)               # class = InputOnly
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 24, 0)               # visual = CopyFromParent
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 28, 0)               # value mask = none
    x_send(&reqbuf[0], 32)
    # NEVER MAPPED. An owner window has no business on screen; the selection
    # protocol needs a window ID, not a visible one.
    return 1


def query_xfixes() -> int32:
    nm: Ptr[uint8] = "XFIXES"
    n: uint64 = 6
    total: uint64 = 8 + pad4(n)
    zero(&reqbuf[0], total)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_QueryExtension
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 4, cast[uint32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        reqbuf[8 + i] = nm[i]
        i = i + 1
    seq: uint32 = x_send(&reqbuf[0], total)
    if x_await_reply(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    if xin[8] == 0:
        return 0
    op: uint32 = cast[uint32](xin[9])
    ev: uint32 = cast[uint32](xin[10])

    # XFixesQueryVersion FIRST -- the extension refuses every other request
    # until the client has told it which version it speaks.
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 12)
    reqbuf[0] = cast[uint8](op)
    reqbuf[1] = 0                          # minor opcode 0
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 3)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, 5)                # client major 5
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, 0)                # client minor 0
    seq = x_send(&reqbuf[0], 12)
    if x_await_reply(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    xfixes_op = op
    xfixes_event = ev
    return 1


def xfixes_watch(sel: uint32):
    # 16 bytes, length 4: header(4) window(4) selection(4) eventMask(4). It was
    # 20/5 here for one measured run, and the whole X-to-Hamnix direction was
    # dead with nothing to show for it but `X error code 16 for request opcode
    # 138` -- BadLength, on a request with no reply to carry the failure. That
    # line existing at all is why this took minutes instead of an afternoon.
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 16)
    reqbuf[0] = cast[uint8](xfixes_op)
    reqbuf[1] = 2                          # XFixesSelectSelectionInput
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 4)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, mywin)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, sel)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, 7)               # SetOwner|WindowDestroy|ClientClose
    x_send(&reqbuf[0], 16)


def get_selection_owner(sel: uint32) -> uint32:
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 8)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_GetSelectionOwner
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 2)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, sel)
    seq: uint32 = x_send(&reqbuf[0], 8)
    if x_await_reply(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    return get32(&xin[0], 8)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- /dev/snarf

def snarf_path(which: int32) -> Ptr[char]:
    if which == 0:
        return cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/snarf")
    return cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/snarf.primary")


# Read a whole clipboard buffer into snbuf. Returns its length, or -1 if the
# device is not there -- which is the honest answer when this binary is run
# somewhere the Hamnix runtime does not serve it.
def snarf_read(which: int32) -> int64:
    fd: int32 = sys_open(snarf_path(which))
    if fd < 0:
        return -1
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < SNARF_MAX:
        n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &snbuf[got], SNARF_MAX - got)
        if n <= 0:
            break
        got = got + cast[uint64](n)
    sys_close(fd)
    return cast[int64](got)


def snarf_write(which: int32, src: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(snarf_path(which))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    # A single write at offset 0 REPLACES, and a 0-byte write CLEARS -- the
    # device's rule (lib/devsnarf.ad), which is why an empty X selection can
    # be mirrored faithfully instead of leaving the old text behind.
    if n == 0:
        sys_write(fd, src, 0)
        sys_close(fd)
        return 1
    done: uint64 = 0
    while done < n:
        k: int64 = sys_write(fd, &src[done], n - done)
        if k <= 0:
            sys_close(fd)
            return 0
        done = done + cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(fd)
    return 1


def same_bytes(a: Ptr[uint8], alen: uint64, b: Ptr[uint8], blen: uint64) -> int32:
    if alen != blen:
        return 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < alen:
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    return 1


def copy_bytes(dst: Ptr[uint8], src: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        dst[i] = src[i]
        i = i + 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pulling

# Ask the current owner for `sel` as `target` and leave the bytes in xferbuf.
# >= 0 : that many bytes.  -1 : refused / no answer.  -2 : an INCR transfer.
def convert_and_get(sel: uint32, target: uint32) -> int64:
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 24)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_ConvertSelection
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 6)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, mywin)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, sel)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, target)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 16, atom_prop)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 20, 0)               # time = CurrentTime
    x_send(&reqbuf[0], 24)

    # The answer is an EVENT, not a reply, so it carries no sequence number to
    # match: wait for a SelectionNotify naming this selection. Bounded, because
    # an owner that never answers is a real thing (a client that died between
    # taking ownership and being asked) and it must not wedge the bridge.
    budget: int32 = 30                     # three seconds
    prop: uint32 = 0
    got_notify: int32 = 0
    while budget > 0 and got_notify == 0:
        r: int32 = x_poll_msg(100)
        if r < 0:
            return -1
        if r == 0:
            budget = budget - 1
            continue
        if evtype() == EV_SelectionNotify and get32(&xin[0], 12) == sel:
            prop = get32(&xin[0], 20)
            got_notify = 1
        else:
            dispatch()
    if got_notify == 0:
        sayerr("the X selection owner did not answer in three seconds\n")
        return -1
    if prop == 0:
        return -1                          # the owner refused this target

    # delete = 1: take it and leave the owner's window clean.
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 24)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_GetProperty
    reqbuf[1] = 1
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 6)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, mywin)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, prop)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, 0)               # AnyPropertyType
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 16, 0)               # long offset
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 20, 16384)           # 16384 * 4 == SNARF_MAX
    seq: uint32 = x_send(&reqbuf[0], 24)
    if x_await_reply(seq) == 0:
        return -1
    fmt: uint32   = cast[uint32](xin[1])
    ptype: uint32 = get32(&xin[0], 8)
    after: uint32 = get32(&xin[0], 12)
    vlen: uint32  = get32(&xin[0], 16)
    if ptype == atom_incr:
        return -2
    if vlen == 0:
        return 0
    if fmt != 8:
        sayerr("the X selection answered with a non-byte format; refusing it\n")
        return -1
    n: uint64 = cast[uint64](vlen)
    if n > rep_extra:
        n = rep_extra
    if after > 0:
        sayerr("the X selection is larger than the 64 KiB clipboard: TRUNCATED, ")
        putd(2, cast[uint64](after))
        puts(2, " bytes dropped\n")
    return cast[int64](n)


def pull(sel: uint32, which: int32):
    n: int64 = convert_and_get(sel, atom_utf8)
    if n == -1:
        # An owner that cannot do UTF8_STRING is usually an old client that
        # can do STRING. Ask once more before giving up.
        n = convert_and_get(sel, ATOM_STRING)
    if n == -2:
        sayerr("the X selection is an INCR (incremental) transfer, which this bridge does not receive; the Hamnix clipboard is UNCHANGED\n")
        return
    if n < 0:
        sayerr("could not read the X selection; the Hamnix clipboard is UNCHANGED\n")
        return

    ln: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
    dst: Ptr[uint8] = &clipbuf[0]
    if which == 1:
        dst = &primbuf[0]
    copy_bytes(dst, &xferbuf[0], ln)
    # THE CACHE IS UPDATED BEFORE THE DEVICE IS WRITTEN, and that ordering is
    # the whole anti-ping-pong invariant: the next poll of /dev/snarf must not
    # see our own write as a Hamnix-side change and go claiming the selection
    # back off the X client that just gave it to us.
    if which == 0:
        clip_len = ln
    else:
        prim_len = ln
    if snarf_write(which, dst, ln) == 0:
        sayerr("could not write /dev/snarf -- is this binary running where the Hamnix runtime serves it?\n")
        return
    say("X -> Hamnix: ")
    putd(1, ln)
    puts(1, " bytes into ")
    if which == 0:
        puts(1, "/dev/snarf\n")
    else:
        puts(1, "/dev/snarf.primary\n")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- claiming

def claim(sel: uint32, which: int32) -> int32:
    zero(&reqbuf[0], 16)
    reqbuf[0] = OP_SetSelectionOwner
    put16(&reqbuf[0], 2, 4)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 4, mywin)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 8, sel)
    put32(&reqbuf[0], 12, 0)               # time = CurrentTime
    x_send(&reqbuf[0], 16)
    # ASK WHETHER IT WORKED. SetSelectionOwner has no reply, so a claim that
    # the server dropped (a dead window, a bad atom) is otherwise indis-
    # tinguishable from a claim that took -- and the symptom is a paste that
    # quietly produces the wrong text for the rest of the session.
    owner: uint32 = get_selection_owner(sel)
    if owner != mywin:
        sayerr("SetSelectionOwner did NOT take; the X side still has another owner\n")
        return 0
    if which == 0:
        own_clip = 1
    else:
        own_prim = 1
    say("Hamnix -> X: owning ")
    if which == 0:
        puts(1, "CLIPBOARD with ")
        putd(1, clip_len)
    else:
        puts(1, "PRIMARY with ")
        putd(1, prim_len)
    puts(1, " bytes\n")
    return 1


# Has the Hamnix side changed under us? Compare bytes -- see the header note
# about the missing serial.
def snarf_tick():
    n: int64 = snarf_read(0)
    if n >= 0:
        ln: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
        if same_bytes(&snbuf[0], ln, &clipbuf[0], clip_len) == 0:
            copy_bytes(&clipbuf[0], &snbuf[0], ln)
            clip_len = ln
            claim(atom_clipboard, 0)
    n = snarf_read(1)
    if n >= 0:
        ln2: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
        if same_bytes(&snbuf[0], ln2, &primbuf[0], prim_len) == 0:
            copy_bytes(&primbuf[0], &snbuf[0], ln2)
            prim_len = ln2
            claim(ATOM_PRIMARY, 1)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- session

# Everything that has to happen once per X connection. 0 = give up on this
# connection and redial.
def x_session_start() -> int32:
    atom_clipboard = intern_atom("CLIPBOARD")
    atom_utf8      = intern_atom("UTF8_STRING")
    atom_targets   = intern_atom("TARGETS")
    atom_text      = intern_atom("TEXT")
    atom_incr      = intern_atom("INCR")
    atom_prop      = intern_atom("HAMNIX_SNARF")
    if atom_clipboard == 0 or atom_utf8 == 0 or atom_targets == 0:
        sayerr("could not intern the selection atoms\n")
        return 0
    create_window()
    if query_xfixes() == 0:
        sayerr("WARNING no XFIXES on this X server. Handovers BETWEEN TWO OTHER X clients will be invisible to this bridge -- it will only notice the ones that take the selection away from IT.\n")
    else:
        xfixes_watch(atom_clipboard)
        xfixes_watch(ATOM_PRIMARY)

    # WHO WINS AT STARTUP, said once rather than left to whoever races.
    # A live X owner holds content that is on somebody's screen right now, so
    # it wins; an unowned selection has nothing to lose, so the Hamnix
    # clipboard is published into it.
    i: int32 = 0
    while i < 2:
        sel: uint32 = atom_clipboard
        if i == 1:
            sel = ATOM_PRIMARY
        owner: uint32 = get_selection_owner(sel)
        if owner != 0:
            pull(sel, i)
        else:
            have: uint64 = clip_len
            if i == 1:
                have = prim_len
            if have > 0:
                claim(sel, i)
        i = i + 1
    if xdead != 0:
        return 0
    return 1


def set_from(dst: Ptr[uint8], src: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while src[i] != 0 and i < cap - 1:
        dst[i] = src[i]
        i = i + 1
    dst[i] = 0


def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    if argc < 2:
        puts(2, "usage: xsnarfd <x11-socket-path> [tag]\n")
        puts(2, "  e.g. xsnarfd /n/alpine/tmp/.X11-unix/X0 alpine\n")
        return 2
    set_from(&sockpath[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[1]), 512)
    if argc > 2:
        set_from(&tagbuf[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[2]), 64)
    else:
        set_from(&tagbuf[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("x"), 64)

    say("bridging ")
    puts(1, &sockpath[0])
    puts(1, " <-> /dev/snarf\n")

    # PROVE THE HAMNIX HALF BEFORE CLAIMING ANYTHING. If /dev/snarf is not
    # served here there is no bridge to build, and saying so now beats an X
    # session that works perfectly and mirrors into nothing.
    if snarf_read(0) < 0:
        sayerr("/dev/snarf is NOT served in this process; nothing to bridge. (This must run as a Hamnix binary, outside the distribution namespace.)\n")
        return 1
    clip_len = cast[uint64](snarf_read(0))
    copy_bytes(&clipbuf[0], &snbuf[0], clip_len)
    prim_len = cast[uint64](snarf_read(1))
    copy_bytes(&primbuf[0], &snbuf[0], prim_len)

    quiet: int32 = 0
    while 1 == 1:
        if xfd < 0:
            if x_connect(&sockpath[0]) == 0 or x_session_start() == 0:
                x_drop()
                # The X server inside a namespace is started by the session,
                # not by the boot, so "not there yet" is the NORMAL state for
                # as long as nobody has launched a program. Say it once and
                # then once a minute, rather than a line a second for ever.
                if quiet == 0:
                    say("no X server at that socket yet; retrying every second\n")
                quiet = quiet + 1
                if quiet >= 60:
                    quiet = 0
                sys_waitfds(&waitset[0], 0, 1000)
                continue
            quiet = 0

        r: int32 = x_poll_msg(200)
        if r < 0:
            sayerr("the X connection went away; redialling\n")
            x_drop()
            continue
        while r == 1:
            dispatch()
            r = x_poll_msg(0)
        if r < 0:
            sayerr("the X connection went away; redialling\n")
            x_drop()
            continue

        # Deferred because a pull is a round trip that can serve other events
        # in the middle of it, and doing that from inside dispatch() would
        # nest the message pump inside itself.
        if pull_clip != 0:
            pull_clip = 0
            pull(atom_clipboard, 0)
        if pull_prim != 0:
            pull_prim = 0
            pull(ATOM_PRIMARY, 1)
        if xdead != 0:
            sayerr("the X connection went away; redialling\n")
            x_drop()
            continue

        snarf_tick()
        if xdead != 0:
            sayerr("the X connection went away; redialling\n")
            x_drop()
    return 0
